TP.register({id:"lp6-g4",lp:6,grade:"4",mode:"curriculum",checks:5,nextGrade:"5th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const CREW_TITLE="The Rope Crew";const CREW="The rope crew works in the hour before dark, when the light goes flat and gray. There are nine of them this year, and every one has a job.\n\nLucia walks the whole length of the rope first, hand over hand, feeling for frayed spots. Anything rough gets a strip of orange tape so the rest of the crew knows to skip that hook.\n\nBehind her come the hangers. Each one carries a canvas bag of paper lanterns and a coil of cord. They work from the middle of the bridge outward, two to a side, and they never pass a hook until a lantern is tied onto it.\n\nLast comes Omar with the long pole and the lighter. He does not hurry. A lantern lit too early burns down before the walk begins, so he waits for Lucia's call from the far end, and only then does he start.\n\nBy the time the last one catches, the river underneath is carrying a second rope of light, upside down and a little blurry.";const HANA_TITLE="The Long Way Round";const HANA="Hana had asked for the rope job three years running, and three years running her mother had said she was too young. This year her mother did not say anything at all. She just handed Hana the canvas bag and the orange tape.\n\nThe bag was heavier than it looked. Hana set it down twice on the way across the bridge, and both times she picked it up again before anyone could offer to help.\n\nAt hook forty-one the cord was too short to reach. She stood there with the lantern in both hands, thinking. Then she untied the lantern at hook forty, swapped the two cords, and tied them both again. It took her four minutes and nobody noticed.\n\nWalking home she kept her hands in her pockets. Her palms had two red lines across them where the bag handle had been, and she was not going to show anyone, because then her mother would carry the bag next year.";const WIND_TITLE="Give It Some Slack";const WIND="The wind came up just after the crew hoisted the last lantern, and the whole rope began to sway.\n\n“Give it some slack,” called Dmitri from the east end. “A rope pulled tight is a rope that snaps.”\n\nLucia let out an arm's length of line, and the sway turned into a slow roll, like a long jump rope turning when two people swing it easy. The lanterns rode it out.\n\nBy dusk the wind had wound down to nothing. A flood of light lay across the bridge, steady now, and the crew finally sat on the cold stone with their backs against the rail.\n\n“That,” said Dmitri, “is why we hang them from the middle out.”";const SKY_TITLE="Reading the Night Sky";const SKY="If you go outside at the same hour every month and look south, the stars will not be in the same place. They shift a little each night. Over a year the whole pattern comes back around to where it started.\n\nThe reason is that Earth is moving. Earth spins once a day, which is why stars seem to cross the sky each night. Earth also travels all the way around the sun once a year. From the ground, that means we face a slightly different part of space each month.\n\nSailors used this long before there were good maps of the open ocean. A sailor who knew which stars rise in which season could tell direction and time of year from the sky alone. Some star patterns are only above the horizon for part of the year, so seeing one is itself a clue about when you are.";const T1={title:"A Lantern on the Bridge",src:"from <i>Light and Distance</i>, a book by Imani Barnes",body:"Stand next to a paper lantern and the light is easy to read by. Walk twenty steps away and the same lantern is only a small spot. The lantern did not get weaker. Its light spread out. Light leaves a lantern in every direction at once. Close up, a lot of that light lands on you. Farther away, the same light has to cover a much wider circle, so only a little of it reaches your eyes. Double the distance and the light that reaches you drops to about one quarter as much."};const T2={title:"Why Some Stars Look Brighter",src:"“Two Kinds of Bright,” a magazine article by Theo Vance",body:"Two stars in the same patch of sky can look very different. One looks bright and one looks faint. There are two reasons a star can look bright: it gives off a great deal of light, or it is close to us. Astronomers measure both. The light a star actually gives off is called its true brightness. How bright it looks from Earth is a different number, because distance changes it. Some of the faintest dots in the sky turn out to be huge stars that are simply very far away, and a few of the brightest points are smaller stars that happen to be nearby."};const T3={title:"Measuring the Distance",src:"“How Far Is That Star?”, a science website page by Rosa Lindqvist",body:"To know how bright a star really is, astronomers first have to know how far away it is. One way is to watch a nearby star from two sides of Earth's path around the sun, six months apart. Against the far background the star seems to shift a tiny amount, and the size of that shift gives its distance. Once the distance is known, the brightness we see can be turned into the brightness the star actually gives off."};const STORE_READ=["Packing the Lanterns Away.","Every lantern comes down the morning after the walk. The paper shells are folded flat and stacked in crates. A folded shell takes up about a tenth of the space an open one does, so the whole set fits in the storage room under the stairs.","Shells that are torn go into a separate crate. Over the winter, two neighbors patch them with rice paper and a thin brush. A patched shell is good for about three more years."].join("\n\n");const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const bullets=(...x)=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${x.map(s=>`<li>${s}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at ${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const twoTexts=(a,b)=>`<div class="texts"><article><h3>Text 1: ${a.title}</h3><p class="small" style="margin:0 0 6px">${a.src}</p><p>${a.body}</p></article><article><h3>Text 2: ${b.title}</h3><p class="small" style="margin:0 0 6px">${b.src}</p><p>${b.body}</p></article></div>`;const threeTexts=()=>`<div class="texts">${[T1,T2,T3].map((t,i)=>`<article><h3>Text ${i+1}: ${t.title}</h3><p>${t.body}</p></article>`).join("")}</div>`;const CONV_TABLE=()=>F.table(["Meters of rope","Centimeters of rope"],[["1","100"],["2","?"],["3","?"],["5","?"]],{caption:"Rope conversion table"});const CREW_TABLE=()=>F.table(["What the rope crew does","Why it is done that way"],[["Walk the whole rope first and tape the frayed spots","A worn rope can part under the weight of the lanterns"],["Hang lanterns from the middle of the bridge outward","The load stays even on both halves of the rope"],["Never pass a hook until a lantern is tied onto it","A skipped hook leaves a gap nobody comes back for"],["Light the lanterns last, and slowly","A lantern lit too early burns out before the walk begins"],["Leave slack in the rope on a windy night","A rope pulled tight is the one that snaps"],["Keep a written count of the lanterns going up","So the crew knows every one of them comes back down"]],{caption:"How the rope crew works"});const SHAPES=[["A",[[10,10],[90,10],[90,90],[10,90]]],["B",[[10,90],[10,20],[80,90]]],["C",[[10,80],[40,25],[95,25],[65,80]]],["D",[[25,20],[80,20],[100,85],[5,85]]],["E",[[8,88],[52,15],[98,70]]]];const shapeRow=()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:18px;align-items:flex-end">${SHAPES.map(s=>group(F.poly(s[1],{size:168,alt:"Flat panel shape "+s[0]}),"Shape "+s[0])).join("")}</div>`;const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Read the Rope Crew",short:"Read Aloud",parts:[{std:["RF.4.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:110,dir:"Read the story out loud. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level story read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(CREW,CREW_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute and mark errors and self-corrections on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Winter grade 4 benchmark: about 120 words correct per minute (Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017, winter 50th percentile). 25th percentile is about 95.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for the pauses at the commas in the long sentence about the hangers, and for a change of pace at “He does not hurry.”"]}]},{id:2,title:"Times As Many",short:"Times As",parts:[{std:["4.OA.A.1"],dur:140,dir:"Answer out loud. For numbers 1, 3, and 4, write the equation on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"reading a multiplication equation as a comparison and writing comparisons as equations",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The town hangs lanterns on two bridges. The small bridge is short. The long bridge crosses the whole river.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.array(1,8,"square"),"Small bridge: 8 lanterns"],[`<p class="huge" style="margin:0">48</p>`,"Long bridge: 48 lanterns"]),qs:["The small bridge has 8 lanterns and the long bridge has 48. The long bridge has how many times as many lanterns? Write the multiplication equation.","Read 56 = 7 × 8 as a comparison. Say it two different ways.","A plain paper lantern takes 5 minutes to fold. A big star lantern takes 4 times as long. Write a multiplication equation and find the time.","One rope of lights is 9 meters long. A second rope is 7 times as long. Write the equation and find the length.","Spot the mistake. Dev says that 36 = 9 × 4 means 36 is 9 more than 4. Fix his sentence and tell him what 9 more than 4 really is."],key:["6 times as many. 48 = 6 × 8. Partial: 40 more is a difference and not a multiplicative comparison, so it does not count. (4.OA.A.1)","56 is 7 times as many as 8, and 56 is 8 times as many as 7. Partial: one of the two ways given is approaching. (4.OA.A.1)","4 × 5 = 20 minutes. Accept t = 4 × 5. Partial: the equation right with no time given is approaching. (4.OA.A.1, a verbal comparison written as a multiplication equation)","7 × 9 = 63 meters. Partial: 63 with no unit named is approaching. (4.OA.A.1)","36 is 9 times as many as 4, not 9 more than 4. 9 more than 4 is 13. Partial: fixing the sentence with no value for 9 more than 4 is approaching. (4.OA.A.1, telling a multiplicative comparison from an additive one)"]}]},{id:3,title:"The Canvas Bag",short:"Canvas Bag",parts:[{std:["RL.4.1"],dur:125,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud and point to the details that prove your answer.",desc:"story questions answered with details and examples from the text",html:()=>F.passage(HANA,HANA_TITLE,true),qs:["How many years had Hana asked for the rope job before this one? Use a detail from the story.","How do you know the bag was heavy? Give two details from the story.","What problem did Hana meet at hook forty-one, and how did she solve it? Use details from the story.","Why did Hana keep her hands in her pockets on the way home? Use the story to explain."],key:["Three. The story says she had asked three years running and her mother said she was too young each time. (RL.4.1, explicit)","Any two: “The bag was heavier than it looked,” she set it down twice on the way across the bridge, and her palms had two red lines where the bag handle had been. (RL.4.1, details)","The cord at hook forty-one was too short to reach. She untied the lantern at hook forty, swapped the two cords, and tied them both again. (RL.4.1, explicit)","So nobody would see the red lines on her palms. The story gives the reason: if anyone saw, her mother would carry the bag next year, and Hana wants the job. (RL.4.1, explicit reason plus inference)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Pack the Measures",short:"Measures",parts:[{std:["4.MD.A.1"],dur:130,dir:"Say each answer out loud. For number 6, read the three missing numbers from the table.",desc:"relative sizes of measurement units and changing a larger unit into a smaller one",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Everything the crew carries up to the bridge gets written down in the smaller unit, so the numbers are whole.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${CONV_TABLE()}</div>`,qs:["A rope is 4 meters long. How many centimeters is that?","A crate holds 3 kilograms of candles. How many grams is that?","The cocoa pot holds 6 liters. How many milliliters is that?","The walk lasts 2 hours. How many minutes is that?","A banner is 5 feet long. How many inches is that?","Finish the table. What are the three missing numbers?"],key:["400 centimeters. 1 meter is 100 centimeters, so 4 × 100 = 400. Partial: 40 uses tens instead of hundreds and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","3,000 grams. 1 kilogram is 1,000 grams, so 3 × 1,000 = 3,000. Partial: 300 uses hundreds instead of thousands and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","6,000 milliliters. 1 liter is 1,000 milliliters, so 6 × 1,000 = 6,000. Partial: 600 uses hundreds instead of thousands and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","120 minutes. 1 hour is 60 minutes, so 2 × 60 = 120. Partial: 120 with no unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.1)","60 inches. 1 foot is 12 inches, so 5 × 12 = 60. Partial: 60 with no unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.1)","200, 300, and 500 centimeters. The pairs are (1, 100), (2, 200), (3, 300), (5, 500). Partial: two of the three missing numbers right is approaching. (4.MD.A.1, a two-column conversion table)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Reading the Night Sky",short:"Sky Text",parts:[{std:["RI.4.1"],dur:115,dir:"Read the text to yourself. Then answer out loud using details from the text.",desc:"informational text questions answered with details and examples from the text",html:()=>F.passage(SKY,SKY_TITLE,true),qs:["Why do the stars seem to cross the sky each night? Use a detail from the text.","Why do we face a slightly different part of space each month? Use the text.","How could a sailor tell the time of year from the sky? Use details and examples from the text.","A sailor's star map drawn in January would not help much in July. How do you know that from the text?"],key:["Because Earth spins once a day. The text names that as the reason. (RI.4.1, explicit)","Because Earth travels all the way around the sun once a year, so from the ground we face a slightly different part of space each month. (RI.4.1, explicit)","A sailor who knew which stars rise in which season could read the season off the sky, and some star patterns are only above the horizon for part of the year, so seeing one is itself a clue about when you are. (RI.4.1, explicit with examples)","The text says the stars shift a little each night and the whole pattern only comes back around after a year, and that some patterns are not above the horizon at all for part of the year. So six months later the sky over the same spot would look different from the January map. (RI.4.1, inference backed by details)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Around the Square",short:"Area",parts:[{std:["4.MD.A.3"],dur:150,dir:"Solve each one on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit with every answer.",desc:"area and perimeter formulas for rectangles in real problems",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The town square by the bridge is a rectangle. The crew measures it before the walk so they know how much rail and how many tiles to order.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.rect(24,15,"m"),"The town square"]),qs:["The square is 24 meters long and 15 meters wide. What is its area?","What is the perimeter of the square?","A banner has an area of 42 square feet and is 6 feet tall. How long is it?","Rug A is 9 feet by 4 feet. Rug B is 6 feet by 6 feet. Which rug has the bigger area?","Which of those two rugs has the smaller perimeter, and by how much?","A rail runs around all four sides of the square. The rail comes in 6 meter pieces. How many pieces does the town need?"],key:["360 square meters. 24 × 15 = 360. Partial: 360 with no square unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","78 meters. 2 × (24 + 15) = 2 × 39 = 78. Or: 24 + 15 + 24 + 15 = 78. Partial: 39 adds only one of each side and does not count. (4.MD.A.3)","7 feet. The area formula with an unknown factor: 6 × ? = 42, so ? = 7. Partial: 7 with no unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","Neither. Both are 36 square feet, because 9 × 4 = 36 and 6 × 6 = 36. Partial: naming neither with only one area worked out is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","Rug B, by 2 feet. Rug A: 2 × (9 + 4) = 26 feet. Rug B: 2 × (6 + 6) = 24 feet. Same area, different perimeter. Partial: naming Rug B with no difference given is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","13 pieces. The perimeter is 78 meters and 78 ÷ 6 = 13. Partial: the perimeter right with no division done is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Give It Some Slack",short:"Word Work",parts:[{std:["RL.4.4"],dur:120,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Tell which words in the story helped you.",desc:"the meaning of words and phrases as they are used in a story",html:()=>F.passage(WIND,WIND_TITLE,true),qs:["What does hoisted mean in the first sentence? What in the story helped you know?","What does slack mean when Dmitri says “Give it some slack”? Use the story.","What does the phrase “a flood of light” mean here? Is there really a flood?","What does it mean that the wind “had wound down to nothing”?","What time of day is dusk? Which words in the story help you know?"],key:["Lifted or raised into place. The crew was putting lanterns up on the rope, and the rope started to sway right after, so hoisted has to mean they raised the last one up. (RL.4.4)","Looseness in the rope, the opposite of pulled tight. Dmitri says “A rope pulled tight is a rope that snaps,” and Lucia answers by letting out an arm's length of line. (RL.4.4)","There is no real flood. It means so much light that it spread out over the whole bridge, the way water spreads when it floods. (RL.4.4, nonliteral phrase)","The wind slowed down little by little until it stopped. The next words, “steady now,” show the swaying had ended. (RL.4.4)","The time just after the sun goes down, when it is getting dark. The crew started in the hour before dark, the lanterns are already lit, and by dusk the light lies across the bridge. (RL.4.4)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Sort the Panels",short:"Panels",parts:[{std:["4.G.A.2"],dur:165,dir:"Look at the five flat panels. Answer out loud. Point to the sides or the corners you mean.",desc:"classifying two-dimensional figures by parallel sides, perpendicular sides, and angle size",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">A paper lantern is built from flat panels cut in different shapes. Parallel sides run the same direction and never meet. Perpendicular sides meet at a square corner, which is a right angle.</p>`,fig:()=>shapeRow(),qs:["Which panels have at least one pair of parallel sides? Name them.","Which panels have perpendicular sides? Name them.","Which panel is a right triangle? How do you know?","Panel E is a triangle too. Is it a right triangle? How do you know?","Panel D has exactly one pair of parallel sides. Panel C has two pairs. What is each of those shapes called?","Name a panel that has an angle bigger than a right angle. Point to that angle."],key:["A, C, and D. Panel A is a square, so both pairs of opposite sides are parallel. Panel C is a parallelogram with two pairs. Panel D has one pair, the top side and the bottom side. Panels B and E are triangles, and a triangle has no parallel sides. Partial: two of the three panels named is approaching. (4.G.A.2)","A and B. Panel A has four square corners, so every pair of sides that meet is perpendicular. Panel B has one square corner, at its lower left. C, D, and E have no square corners. Partial: one of the two panels named is approaching. (4.G.A.2)","Panel B. It is a triangle with one right angle, at the lower left corner where the upright side meets the bottom side. Partial: naming Panel B with no reason is approaching. (4.G.A.2, identifies right triangles)","No. None of Panel E's three corners is a square corner. Checked with a corner of paper or an index card, every one of them is either wider or narrower than a right angle. Partial: saying no with no check of the corners is approaching. (4.G.A.2)","Panel D is a trapezoid and Panel C is a parallelogram. Partial: one of the two names given is approaching. (4.G.A.2)","Panel D or Panel C. Panel D's two upper corners are wider than a right angle, and so are two of Panel C's corners, the upper left one and the lower right one. Panels A, B, and E have no angle bigger than a right angle. Partial: naming a panel with no angle pointed out is approaching. (4.G.A.2, angles of a specified size)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Two Kinds of Bright",short:"Two Texts",parts:[{std:["RI.4.9"],n:3,dur:140,dir:"Read both texts to yourself. Then answer out loud. Use a fact from each text in every answer.",desc:"putting together information from two texts on the same topic",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Both texts below are about the same question: why does a light look as bright as it does?</p>${twoTexts(T1,T2)}`,qs:["Use both texts. A star looks faint from Earth. Explain why that does not have to mean it is a small star.","Use both texts. A paper lantern on the bridge can look brighter than a star. That star gives off far more light. Explain why it looks brighter to you.","Text 1 says that doubling the distance drops the light to about one quarter as much. How does that fact back up what Text 2 says about distance?"],key:["From Text 1: light spreads out as it travels, so the farther away a light is, the less of it reaches your eyes, and it does not mean the light got weaker. From Text 2: distance changes how bright a star looks, and some of the faintest dots turn out to be huge stars that are simply very far away. Credit an answer that uses at least one fact from each text. (RI.4.9)","From Text 1: close up, a lot of a lantern's light lands on you, while far away the same light has to cover a much wider circle. From Text 2: how bright a star looks depends on both the light it gives off and how close it is, and stars are enormously far away. (RI.4.9)","Text 2 only says that distance changes how bright something looks. Text 1 puts a size on it: go twice as far and only about a quarter of the light reaches you, so light falls off fast. That is why a star hundreds of times farther away can look faint even though it gives off far more light. (RI.4.9, integrating a detail from one text into the point of the other)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Star Notes",short:"Notes",parts:[{std:["W.4.8"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:105,dir:"Read the two sources. Write your notes on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"taking notes in their own words, sorting the notes, and listing the sources",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">You are gathering facts for a report on why some lights look brighter than others.</p>${twoTexts(T1,T2)}`,qs:["Write three notes. Use both sources. Keep them short and use your own words, not the sentences from the page.","Sort your notes. Write a heading above each one: How Distance Changes Light, or What Makes a Star Bright.","List your sources. Next to each note, write the title of the source it came from."],key:["Three short notes drawn from both sources, in the student's own words and not copied sentences. Examples: light spreads out as it goes; twice as far means about a quarter the light; a star can look bright because it is close; the faintest dots can be huge far stars. (W.4.8, gathers information, takes notes, paraphrases)","Each note sits under the heading that fits. Spreading out, the wider circle, and the one quarter rule go under How Distance Changes Light. True brightness, how bright it looks from Earth, and faint huge stars go under What Makes a Star Bright. (W.4.8, categorizes)","Writes both titles next to the right notes: A Lantern on the Bridge, and “Two Kinds of Bright.” Accept the authors as well. (W.4.8, provides a list of sources)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Fix the Crew Log",short:"Grammar",parts:[{std:["L.4.1"],dur:90,dir:"Say each line out loud the correct way.",desc:"spoken grammar items (relative pronouns, progressive tense, modal words, order of adjectives, run-ons, confused words)",qs:["The neighbor ____ ties the knots has done the job for ten years. (who or which)","Right now the crew ____ the rope across the bridge. (pull) Start with is.","Pick the better word: The rope is not tied yet. We ____ let go of it. (must not or might not)","Fix the order of the words: a red small paper lantern","Fix this run-on: The sun went down we lit the first lantern.","Finish with there, their, or they're: The families left ____ lanterns on the rope. ____ coming back for them in the morning."],key:["who. A relative pronoun for a person. (L.4.1)","is pulling. (L.4.1, progressive verb tense)","must not. The rope is not tied, so letting go is not allowed, not just unlikely. (L.4.1, modal auxiliary)","a small red paper lantern. Size, then color, then what it is made of. (L.4.1, ordering adjectives)","Any correct fix, such as: The sun went down, so we lit the first lantern. Or two sentences: The sun went down. We lit the first lantern. (L.4.1, correcting a run-on)","their lanterns, and They're coming back for them. (L.4.1, frequently confused words)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Bridge Writer",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Explain",icon:"📋",hint:"How the rope crew works"},std:["W.4.2"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:195,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">How does the rope crew get a hundred lanterns onto the bridge safely? Write to explain it. Use facts from the chart. Write 5 to 7 sentences.</p>${bullets("Introduce your topic","Give facts, details, or examples","Use linking words: for example, also, because","End with a concluding sentence")}<div class="figure">${CREW_TABLE()}</div>`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly (W.4.2)","Develops the topic with facts and details from the chart, such as walking the rope first and taping frayed spots, hanging from the middle outward, never passing a hook, lighting last and slowly, leaving slack on a windy night, or keeping a written count (W.4.2)","Links ideas with words such as for example, also, because, another (W.4.2)","Ends with a concluding statement (W.4.2)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"✨",hint:"The last one on the bridge"},std:["W.4.3"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:195,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Every lantern is lit and the crowd has gone home. You are the last person still on the bridge, and you have one job left to finish before you go. Write the story of what happens. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${bullets("Set the scene and tell who is there","Tell the events in a clear order, with time words","Use dialogue or a description of what someone thinks and feels","Use words that help the reader picture the bridge at night","Give the story an ending")}`,key:["Sets up the situation and introduces the narrator or characters (W.4.3)","Events unfold in a clear order, helped by time words such as first, after that, by then, finally (W.4.3)","Uses dialogue or description to show what someone thinks or feels (W.4.3)","Uses concrete words and sensory detail, and ends the story (W.4.3)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Places and Rounding",short:"Place Round",parts:[{title:"Ten Times As Much",std:["4.NBT.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. Tell how you know.",desc:"a digit in one place is worth ten times the same digit in the place to its right",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The county keeps one written count of every lantern hung in every town.</p><p class="huge" style="text-align:center;margin:8px 0">7,700</p>`,qs:["Look at 7,700. The 7 in the thousands place has a value. The 7 in the hundreds place has a value. How do they compare?","The county count one year was 3,330. Say the value of each 3.","Why is 600 ÷ 60 equal to 10? Answer with place value."],key:["It is ten times as much. The first 7 is worth 7,000 and the second is worth 700, and 7,000 is ten times 700. Partial: naming the two values with no comparison is approaching. (4.NBT.A.1)","3,000, then 300, then 30. Each 3 is worth ten times the 3 to its right. Partial: two of the three values given is approaching. (4.NBT.A.1)","Because 600 is ten times 60. Six hundreds is ten times six tens, so it takes ten 60s to make 600. Partial: working the division out with no place value reason is approaching. (4.NBT.A.1)"]},{title:"Round the Count",std:["4.NBT.A.3"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. Tell which digit you looked at.",desc:"rounding multi-digit whole numbers to any place",qs:["Round 6,482 to the nearest hundred.","Round 6,482 to the nearest thousand.","Round 27,350 to the nearest ten thousand."],key:["6,500. Look at the tens digit, 8. It is 5 or more, so the hundreds digit goes up. Partial: 6,400 rounds down when the tens digit is 8 and does not count. (4.NBT.A.3)","6,000. Look at the hundreds digit, 4. It is less than 5, so the thousands digit stays. Partial: 7,000 rounds up when the hundreds digit is 4 and does not count. (4.NBT.A.3)","30,000. Look at the thousands digit, 7. It is 5 or more, so the ten thousands digit goes from 2 to 3. Partial: 20,000 rounds down when the thousands digit is 7 and does not count. (4.NBT.A.3)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Same Amount, Different Pieces",short:"Equal Parts",parts:[{title:"Cut Every Piece",std:["4.NF.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the two banners. Answer out loud. Tell how you know.",desc:"explaining why two fractions are equivalent with a visual model, and generating equivalent fractions",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Banner A and Banner B are exactly the same size.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:28px;align-items:flex-end">${group(F.fracBar(4,3,{width:300}),"Banner A: 3/4 shaded")}${group(F.fracBar(8,6,{width:300}),"Banner B: 6/8 shaded")}</div>`,qs:["Is the same amount of banner shaded on both? Use the picture to explain.","Write 3/4 as an equivalent fraction with 12 as the bottom number.","Multiply the top number and the bottom number by the same number. That does not change how much of the banner is shaded. Why not?"],key:["Yes. Every fourth on Banner A was cut in half to make Banner B, so there are twice as many parts and each part is half as big. The shaded length is the same. 3/4 = 6/8. Partial: saying yes with nothing said about the size of the parts is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","9/12. Multiply the top and the bottom by 3, because each fourth is cut into 3 equal pieces. Partial: 9/12 right with no reason is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","Because cutting every piece into the same number of smaller pieces gives you that many times more shaded pieces and that many times more pieces in the whole. More parts, each one smaller, and the same amount covered. Partial: naming the multiplying with nothing said about the pieces being smaller is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)"]},{title:"Which Is Greater",std:["4.NF.A.2"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Answer out loud. Record each answer with a symbol. Tell how you know.",desc:"comparing fractions with unlike numerators and denominators, and knowing a comparison needs the same whole",qs:["Which is greater, 5/6 or 7/12? Record it with a symbol.","Which is greater, 5/9 or 2/5? Use 1/2 as a benchmark.","Mia says one fourth of her rope is longer than one half of Dev's rope. Can that be true? Can you write 1/4 > 1/2?"],key:["5/6 > 7/12. In twelfths, 5/6 is 10/12, and 10/12 is more than 7/12. Partial: the right choice with the sign the wrong way round is approaching. (4.NF.A.2)","5/9. Half of 9 is 4.5, so 5/9 is more than 1/2. Half of 5 is 2.5, so 2/5 is less than 1/2. Record it as 5/9 > 2/5. Partial: naming 5/9 with only one of the two benchmark checks is approaching. (4.NF.A.2, benchmark fraction)","Yes, it can be true if Mia's rope is much longer than Dev's. But you cannot write 1/4 > 1/2, because a comparison with a symbol only works when both fractions name parts of the same whole. Partial: saying yes with nothing said about why the symbol cannot be written is approaching. (4.NF.A.2, comparisons are valid only on the same whole)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Tenths and Hundredths",short:"Decimals",parts:[{title:"Write It as a Decimal",std:["4.NF.C.6"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the number line. Answer out loud. Write numbers 1 and 2 on your paper.",desc:"writing fractions with denominators 10 and 100 in decimal notation and locating a decimal on a number line",fig:()=>F.row(F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:.1,labelEvery:.1,width:640})),qs:["Write 62/100 as a decimal.","Write 0.4 as a fraction with 10 as the bottom number. Again with 100 as the bottom number.","A cord measures 0.35 meters. Between which two marks on the number line does 0.35 sit, and what fraction is 0.35?"],key:["0.62. Six tenths and two hundredths. Partial: 0.62 right with no place value words is approaching. (4.NF.C.6)","4/10 and 40/100. Both name the same amount. Partial: one of the two fractions given is approaching. (4.NF.C.6)","Between 0.3 and 0.4, halfway between them. 0.35 is 35/100. Partial: the two marks named with no fraction given is approaching. (4.NF.C.6, locating a decimal on a number line)"]},{title:"Which Cord Is Longer",std:["4.NF.C.7"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Answer out loud. Record each answer with a symbol.",desc:"comparing two decimals to hundredths by reasoning about their size",qs:["Write a symbol to make this true: 0.3 ____ 0.27","Which cord is longer, one that is 0.8 meters or one that is 0.75 meters?","Rope A reaches 0.5 of the way across the long bridge. Rope B reaches 0.6 of the way across the short bridge. Can you write 0.6 > 0.5 to say Rope B is longer?"],key:["0.3 > 0.27. Three tenths is thirty hundredths, and 30 hundredths is more than 27 hundredths. A student who says 0.27 is bigger is comparing 27 with 3 and ignoring the places. Partial: the right choice with the sign the wrong way round is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)","The 0.8 meter cord. 0.8 is 0.80, and 80 hundredths is more than 75 hundredths. Partial: naming the cord with no reason from hundredths is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)","No. The two decimals describe different bridges, so they do not refer to the same whole. 0.6 of a short bridge can be a shorter distance than 0.5 of a long one. Partial: saying no with nothing said about the same whole is approaching. (4.NF.C.7, comparisons are valid only on the same whole)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Long Words on the Log",short:"Word Parts",parts:[{std:["RF.4.3"],kind:"words",words:["relighting","unbearable","astronomer","signature","cautiously","misjudge","foreigner","community"],n:8,dur:45,dir:"Read each word out loud. Look for a word part you already know.",desc:"decoding multisyllable words using prefixes, suffixes, roots, and letter-sound patterns",key:["Read each word in order: relighting, unbearable, astronomer, signature, cautiously, misjudge, foreigner, community.","Watch for: the prefixes re-, un-, mis- (relighting, unbearable, misjudge), the suffixes -ing, -able, -ly (relighting, unbearable, cautiously), the root astro in astronomer, the gn spelling in signature, the eign spelling in foreigner, and the four syllables in community. (RF.4.3)","Count a word correct when the student reads it accurately, even after sounding it out in parts. If a word is missed, ask once what part of it the student recognizes."]}]},{id:17,title:"Listen, Then Tell",short:"Listen Tell",parts:[{title:"Say It Your Way",std:["SL.4.2"],n:3,dur:50,read:STORE_READ,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads. Then look at the graph. Answer out loud in your own words.",desc:"paraphrasing a text read aloud and information shown in a graph",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Your teacher will read a short report about what happens to the lanterns after the walk. Then look at the graph of how long each crew job takes.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.barGraph({title:"Minutes each crew job takes",cats:["Check rope","Hang","Light","Take down"],vals:[20,45,15,30],max:60,step:15}),"One evening of work"]),qs:["Put what I read about storing the lanterns into your own words.","Say in your own words what the graph shows.","Use the graph. In your own words, how does the hanging time compare with the lighting time?"],key:["A paraphrase, not a repeat: the lanterns come down the next morning, get folded flat, and fit in a small storage room, and torn ones are patched over the winter. Reciting the sentences word for word is not a paraphrase. (SL.4.2, paraphrasing a text read aloud)","The graph shows how many minutes each of the four crew jobs takes on one evening. Hanging takes the longest and lighting takes the shortest. (SL.4.2, information presented visually and quantitatively)","Hanging takes 45 minutes and lighting takes 15, so hanging takes three times as long, or 30 minutes longer. Either comparison counts. (SL.4.2)"]},{title:"Report on It",std:["SL.4.4"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:45,dir:"Talk for about a minute. Speak clearly and take your time.",desc:"reporting on a topic in an organized way with facts and descriptive details, at an understandable pace",html:()=>`<p class="big">Pick one job on the rope crew and report on it: what the job is, how it is done, and why it matters. Or report on something you know how to do well.</p>${bullets("Say what you are reporting on first","Give facts in an order that makes sense","Add details that support your main point","Speak clearly and slow enough to follow")}`,key:["Organized: names the topic first, then gives the steps or facts in an order a listener can follow. (SL.4.4, organized manner)","Uses appropriate facts and relevant descriptive details that support the main point. Details that wander off the topic do not count. (SL.4.4)","Speaks clearly at an understandable pace, loud enough to hear, with finished sentences. (SL.4.4)"]}]},{id:18,title:"When Should the Walk Start",short:"Opinion",parts:[{std:["W.4.1"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:60,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion piece with a stated point of view, grouped reasons, linking words, and a conclusion",html:()=>`<p class="big">The town has to pick a start time for the night walk. At dusk the sky is still pale, the path is easy to see, and younger children can come. An hour after dark the lanterns look far brighter and the river reflects them, but it is colder and later. Which start time should the town pick? Write 4 or 5 sentences.</p>${bullets("State your opinion in the first sentence","Give two reasons, and keep each reason with its own support","Use linking words: because, for instance, in order to, also","End with a concluding sentence")}`,key:["Introduces the topic and states a clear opinion about the start time (W.4.1)","Supplies reasons that support the point of view, drawn from the information given or from the student's own knowledge (W.4.1)","Groups related ideas together and links them with words such as because, for instance, in order to, also (W.4.1)","Provides a concluding statement related to the opinion (W.4.1)"]}]},{id:19,title:"New Seedlings, New Notebook",short:"Structures",parts:[{std:["4-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the seedling and the cat. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"arguing that named plant and animal structures do a job that supports survival, growth or reproduction",html:()=>`<p class="big">The first page of the new notebook is a bean seedling started in a cup. The cat that sleeps on the window sill beside it.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sprout",104)}${F.icon("cat",104)}</div>
     <p class="big">The seedling has a root going down, a stem, two seed leaves, and a growing tip. The cat has long whiskers, claws that pull back into its paws, and strong muscles along its back.</p>`,qs:["Name the job of the seedling's root and the job of its seed leaves.","Pick one cat structure from the list and build an argument for how it supports survival.","The growing tip of a stem is soft and the root tip is hard and pointed. Explain why each one has the form it does."],key:["The root takes in water and minerals from the soil and anchors the plant. The seed leaves hold the food store that feeds the seedling until true leaves can make food from sunlight. (4-LS1-1)","Whiskers feel what is near the face in the dark, so the cat can move through tight spaces. Claws that pull back stay sharp and out of the way, then grip for climbing and holding. Strong back muscles let it spring. Any one, with a claim and a reason. (4-LS1-1)","The root tip is hard and pointed because it has to push through packed soil. The growing tip of the stem stays soft because it is still adding new cells and growing longer in open air where nothing has to be pushed aside. (4-LS1-1)"]},{std:["4-LS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read what happens, then answer. You may draw your model.",desc:"modelling the path from sense organ to brain to response in an animal",html:()=>`<p class="big">The cat is asleep on the sill. The watering can is set down on the table with a clatter. The cat's ears flick, it opens its eyes, looks at the can, then settles back down and goes to sleep again.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("cat",104)}${F.icon("wateringCan",104)}</div>`,qs:["Name two senses the cat used, and the body part that took in each one.","The cat looked, then went back to sleep. What did its brain do to reach that response?","Build a three step model of the whole path. Then show where the choice was made."],key:["Hearing through the ears, then sight through the eyes. The order matters: the sound came first and the look followed. (4-LS1-2)","It took in the sound, worked out what it was, compared it with what it had heard before, and decided no action was needed. Going back to sleep is a response, chosen by the brain. (4-LS1-2)","Sense organs take in information, the brain processes it and chooses, the body responds. The choice is made in the brain, which is the step this standard is really about. (4-LS1-2)"]}]},{id:20,title:"Behind the School",short:"Rock Layers",parts:[{std:["4-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the cut bank. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"reading rock layers and the fossils in them as evidence of landscape change",html:()=>`<p class="big">A new path was cut into the slope behind the school and left four layers showing. Layers stack with the newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What the layer is</th><th>Fossils in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>top</td><td>brown soil with roots</td><td>oak leaves and acorns</td></tr>
     <tr><td>second</td><td>pale windblown silt</td><td>grass seeds and snail shells</td></tr>
     <tr><td>third</td><td>rounded river cobbles</td><td>pieces of freshwater clam</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bottom</td><td>fine dark mudstone</td><td>sea urchin spines</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which layer formed first, and what in the chart tells you?","The bottom layer holds sea urchin spines. What does that tell you about this hillside long ago?","Use all four layers in order to tell the story of this place."],key:["The dark mudstone at the bottom, because layers build upward and everything above it came later. (4-ESS1-1)","It was under the ocean. Sea urchins live in salt water, so the sea must have covered this spot when that mud was settling. (4-ESS1-1)","The sea covered the spot and left fine mud with sea urchins, then a river ran here and left rounded cobbles and freshwater clams, then wind laid down dry silt with grassland snails, then soil formed and oaks grew. Any account that reads the chart upward and names the changes counts. (4-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["4-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the measurements. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using measurements to compare the rate of erosion under different conditions",html:()=>`<p class="big">The class is starting new garden beds on the slope. They tested four small plots, poured the same two litres of water over each, and weighed the soil caught at the bottom.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plot</th><th>What was on it</th><th>Soil caught</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>bare loose soil</td><td>104 grams</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>bare soil raked into ridges across the slope</td><td>45 grams</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>soil with a cover crop growing in it</td><td>12 grams</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>bare soil, water poured from twice the height</td><td>171 grams</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which plot lost the least soil, and how much less than plot 1?","Plots 1 and 4 had the same bare soil. Explain the difference in their results.","The class wants to protect the new beds this winter. Which plot gives them the best evidence for what to do. What should they do?"],key:["Plot 3 with the cover crop, 12 grams, which is 92 grams less than the 104 grams from bare soil. (4-ESS2-1)","The water in plot 4 fell from twice the height, so it hit the soil harder and faster and knocked more grains loose. Faster moving water erodes more. (4-ESS2-1)","Plot 3. They should plant a cover crop in the beds, because growing roots and leaves cut the soil loss to about a ninth of bare soil. Ridges across the slope, from plot 2, are a sound second answer. (4-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Lines on the Map",short:"Map Patterns",parts:[{std:["4-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart of recorded ground movements. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"interpreting map data to describe patterns in Earth's features",html:()=>`<p class="big">A map shows where small ground movements were recorded in one year across a region, measured in kilometres east of the coast.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("mountain",80)}${F.icon("globe",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Band of the map</th><th>Movements recorded</th><th>What else the map shows there</th></tr>
     <tr><td>0 to 20 km east</td><td>8</td><td>coastal hills</td></tr>
     <tr><td>20 to 40 km east</td><td>96</td><td>a long straight valley running north and south</td></tr>
     <tr><td>40 to 60 km east</td><td>11</td><td>rolling farmland</td></tr>
     <tr><td>60 to 80 km east</td><td>4</td><td>flat valley floor</td></tr>
     <tr><td>80 to 100 km east</td><td>7</td><td>foothills</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the pattern in where the movements happen.","The band with the most movements also has a long straight valley running north and south. What might link those two facts?","A new town is planned at 30 km east. Use the map pattern to say what the planners should know."],key:["They are strongly grouped: 96 of them in the band 20 to 40 kilometres east, with only a handful in every other band. (4-ESS2-2)","A long straight valley running north and south is the kind of surface feature a fault line makes over a long time, and that is where ground movement is recorded. The pattern on the map lines up with the shape of the land. (4-ESS2-2)","It sits in the band where nearly all the movement is recorded, so buildings there should be built to handle ground movement. Reading the pattern and applying it to a new place is what is being checked. (4-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["4-ESS3-2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the two plans. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"generating and comparing solutions that reduce the impact of a natural Earth process",html:()=>`<p class="big">The school hall was built long ago, before anyone built for ground movement. The district can fund one improvement this year.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plan</th><th>What it does</th><th>Cost</th><th>How long the work takes</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: bolt the building frame down to its foundation</td><td>keeps the building from sliding off its base when the ground moves</td><td>medium</td><td>three weeks, hall closed</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: strap the tall shelves and water heater to the walls and fit flexible pipe joints</td><td>stops heavy things toppling and pipes snapping</td><td>low</td><td>two days, hall stays open</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name a third improvement of your own that would reduce the effect of ground movement at school.","Compare plan A and plan B. Which would you fund first. What in the chart supports you?","One of these plans reduces what the ground movement does to the building itself. The other reduces what it does to the people and the things inside. Say which plan is which, and which of those two effects you would reduce first."],key:["Any workable idea: fit catches on cupboard doors, move heavy items to low shelves, add bracing to the walls, plan and practise what to do, keep exits clear, replace a heavy ceiling light with a light fitting. (4-ESS3-2)","Either plan, judged on the chart. Plan A protects the whole building, which is the biggest effect, but costs more and closes the hall for three weeks. Plan B is cheap and quick and prevents the most common injuries and water damage. A student who says do B now and A next year is reasoning well. (4-ESS3-2)","A is the building plan, bolting the frame down to its foundation. B is the people and contents plan, strapping the tall shelves and the water heater and fitting flexible pipe joints. Then either effect may be named first, and the reason is what is rated: a building sliding off its base puts everyone inside at risk at once, while toppling shelves and snapping pipes are the harm that comes in the smaller shakes and the chart says B takes two days without closing the hall. Partial: sorting the two plans onto the two effects with no first choice reasoned is approaching. (4-ESS3-2)"]}]},{id:22,title:"The School Energy Audit",short:"Energy Sources",parts:[{std:["4-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing where energy and fuels come from and how their use affects the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">The class walked the school and listed where its energy comes from.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Use at school</th><th>Energy source</th><th>Natural resource behind it</th><th>One effect on the environment</th></tr>
     <tr><td>lights and computers</td><td>mains electricity</td><td>a mix of natural gas, water behind dams, wind and sunlight</td><td>gases from the gas part, land and rivers changed by the rest</td></tr>
     <tr><td>heating the classrooms</td><td>natural gas</td><td>gas from underground</td><td>gases into the air</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the school bus</td><td>diesel</td><td>oil from underground</td><td>exhaust along the route</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the roof panels on the gym</td><td>sunlight</td><td>sunlight</td><td>roof space used, panels made and later recycled</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Trace the school bus's energy back to a natural resource. Name each step.","The mains electricity comes from a mix of resources. Name which parts of that mix will run out and which will not.","The school adds more roof panels and uses less mains electricity. Name one effect that gets smaller and one new effect that appears."],key:["The bus runs on diesel, diesel is refined from oil, and oil is pumped from underground. Three steps named. (4-ESS3-1)","Natural gas will run out, because there is a fixed amount underground. Water behind dams, wind and sunlight will not, because rain, wind and sun keep arriving. (4-ESS3-1)","Fewer gases from burning natural gas to make the school's electricity. New effects: panels take up roof space, they have to be manufactured and shipped, and they make nothing at night so the school still draws from the mains then. (4-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:23,title:"The Marble Track",short:"Speed",parts:[{std:["4-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the results. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using evidence to explain how the speed of an object relates to its energy",html:()=>`<p class="big">The class built a track and rolled the same marble down from three heights into a small cardboard box, measuring how far the box was pushed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Release height</th><th>Speed at the bottom</th><th>Box pushed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>10 cm</td><td>1.4 metres each second</td><td>2 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>40 cm</td><td>2.8 metres each second</td><td>9 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>90 cm</td><td>4.2 metres each second</td><td>20 cm</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What happens to the push distance as the marble's speed goes up?","Which release had the most energy in the moving marble? Name the evidence.","The speed trebled from the first run to the third. Did the push distance treble? Say what that shows."],key:["It grows, and by bigger steps each time: 2 cm, 9 cm, 20 cm. (4-PS3-1)","The 90 cm release, at 4.2 metres each second, because it pushed the box 20 cm, farther than either other run. (4-PS3-1)","No, it grew about ten times, from 2 cm to 20 cm, while the speed only trebled. Energy grows much faster than speed does. Noticing that the two do not grow together is the point. (4-PS3-1)"]},{std:["4-PS3-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing. A prediction needs a reason, not the right answer.",desc:"asking a question and predicting the energy change in a collision",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two marbles sit in the track groove. A heavy steel marble is rolling along the groove toward a light glass marble that is standing still.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("soccerBall",96)}${F.icon("gem",96)}</div>`,qs:["Ask one question about this collision that a test could answer.","Predict what happens to each marble after they meet.","The class hears a sharp click when they collide. What does the click tell you about the energy?"],key:["Any testable question: how far does the glass marble travel, is the click louder from a faster roll, what happens if the glass marble hits the steel one instead, does a groove lined with felt change the result. (4-PS3-3)","The glass marble is pushed forward and speeds off. The steel marble slows down and carries on more slowly. Both halves make the full prediction. (4-PS3-3)","Some of the energy left the marbles as sound. Not all of the moving energy went into the glass marble: a little became sound, and a little became heat where they touched. (4-PS3-3)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Energy in the Classroom",short:"Transfer",parts:[{std:["4-PS3-2"],n:4,dur:75,dir:"For each observation, say how the energy travelled. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"matching observations to energy transferred by sound, light, heat or electric current",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four things noticed in the classroom in one morning.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>What was noticed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>A cord runs from the wall to the fish tank pump, and the pump starts bubbling.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>The projector is switched on and a bright rectangle appears on the far wall.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>A metal ruler left lying on the heater becomes warm along its whole length.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>The bell rings in the corridor and everybody in the room hears it.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How did the energy travel in observation 1?","How did it travel in observation 2?","How did it travel in observation 3?","How did it travel in observation 4?"],key:["By an electric current, through the cord from the wall to the pump, where it becomes motion and sound. (4-PS3-2)","By light, from the projector across the room to the wall. (4-PS3-2)","By heat, from the heater into the metal and along the ruler. (4-PS3-2)","By sound, through the air from the bell down the corridor and into the room. (4-PS3-2)"]},{std:["4-PS3-4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Draw your device and label it. Then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"designing, testing and improving a device that converts energy from one form to another",html:()=>`<p class="big">The new seedlings need moving air so their stems grow sturdy. There is no spare outlet on the window wall. Design a device that turns one kind of energy into the spinning of a small fan. You may use a small solar panel, wire, a small motor, a hand crank, a rubber band, or a weight on a string.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sun",96)}${F.icon("sprout",96)}</div>`,qs:["Draw and label your device. Show the energy going in and the fan turning at the other end.","Name the kind of energy going in and the kind coming out.","You test it on a cloudy day and the fan barely turns. Name one change and say why it should help."],key:["Any labelled drawing with a path: a solar panel wired to a small motor with a fan blade; a crank or a falling weight turning the fan through a rubber band. Labels and a path through the device make it a design. (4-PS3-4)","Going in: light from the sun, or motion from the crank or the falling weight. Coming out: motion of the fan, plus some sound and heat. Both forms named. (4-PS3-4)","Any change with a reason: a bigger panel or two panels to take in more light, move the panel to the brightest window, add a battery charged on sunny days, use lighter fan blades that need less energy to turn, cut friction in the bearing. (4-PS3-4)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Ropes, Mirrors and a New Code",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["4-PS4-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the two drawings. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using a wave model to describe amplitude and wavelength and the effect waves have on objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two children shook a long rope tied to the wall, and the class drew the shape it made. The dotted line is the rope at rest. A paper ring is threaded on the rope.</p>
     <div class="figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 520 300" width="520" role="img" aria-label="Two rope waves drawn above one another with letters A and B marking parts of the first wave">
       <text x="10" y="18" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">shaken slowly, small movement</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="70" x2="500" y2="70" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>
       <path d="M20 70 q40 -32 80 0 t80 0 t80 0 t80 0 t80 0 t80 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/>
       <line x1="40" y1="70" x2="40" y2="55" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
       <text x="52" y="60" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">A</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="112" x2="180" y2="112" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
       <text x="100" y="134" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">B</text>
       <text x="10" y="186" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">shaken fast, big movement</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="240" x2="500" y2="240" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>
       <path d="M20 240 q20 -70 40 0 t40 0 t40 0 t40 0 t40 0 t40 0 t40 0 t40 0 t40 0 t40 0 t40 0 t40 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/>
     </svg></div>`,qs:["Which letter marks the wavelength, and which marks the amplitude?","Compare the two drawings. Say what happened to the amplitude and what happened to the wavelength.","What does the second wave do to the paper ring threaded on the rope?"],key:["B marks the wavelength, from one crest to the next. A marks the amplitude, from the rest line up to the crest. (4-PS4-1)","The amplitude got bigger, because the crests are taller. The wavelength got smaller, because the crests are much closer together. This is the pair where both change, and in opposite directions. (4-PS4-1)","It makes the ring move up and down farther and more often, because a bigger amplitude carries more energy. The ring moves up and down in place; it does not travel along the rope with the wave. (4-PS4-1)"]},{std:["4-PS4-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing. You may draw arrows.",desc:"modelling how light reflects from objects into the eye so the objects can be seen",html:()=>`<p class="big">A child holds a mirror at an angle above the top of a bookshelf. In it the child can see a pencil lying on top. This happens without seeing the pencil directly.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("pencil",96)}${F.icon("lamp",96)}</div>`,qs:["Draw or tell the whole path of the light from the ceiling lamp to the child's eye. Include the mirror.","The lamp is switched off and the room is completely dark. Why does the mirror show nothing?","The pencil is not making light. How do you know?"],key:["Light leaves the lamp, travels to the pencil, reflects off the pencil, travels to the mirror, reflects off the mirror, and travels into the eye. Two reflections, both named. (4-PS4-2)","There is no light in the room to reflect off the pencil, so no light reaches the mirror and none reaches the eye. A mirror does not make light, it only redirects it. (4-PS4-2)","With the lamp off it cannot be seen at all, in the mirror or directly. Something making its own light would still show in the dark. (4-PS4-2)"]},{std:["4-PS4-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two systems. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"comparing two solutions that use patterns to send information",html:()=>`<p class="big">For the new year the class needs three silent signals from the teacher at the front to a group working at the back: come and show me, ten minutes left, and tidy up.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>System</th><th>How a message is sent</th><th>Needs</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Card on the easel</td><td>a card showing one line, two lines, or three lines</td><td>a clear view of the easel; the group must look up to notice it</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Desk lamp pattern</td><td>one slow flash, two slow flashes, or a steady glow</td><td>a lamp in the group's view; works even if the easel is blocked</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Say what the pattern is in each system.","Both systems are silent. Name one advantage each one has over the other.","Design a third silent system of your own for the same three messages."],key:["In the card system the pattern is the number of lines showing. In the lamp system it is the number of flashes and whether the light is flashing or steady. An agreed pattern standing for a message is what carries the information. (4-PS4-3)","The card needs no power and can be read whenever someone looks up. The lamp catches the eye by changing, so the group notices without having to look up, and it works when something blocks the easel. (4-PS4-3)","Any silent pattern with three agreed signals: three positions of a marker on a wall track, one, two or three coloured blocks stacked on a shelf, a hand signal with one, two or three fingers, a card turned to one of three colours. (4-PS4-3)"]}]},{id:26,title:"A Planter That Waters Itself",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Read the situation, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">The classroom seedlings dry out over a long weekend and some of them die. The class has one afternoon, clean jars, string, plastic bottles, a tray, and no budget.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sprout",88)}${F.icon("drop",88)}</div>`,qs:["State the problem in one sentence, in your own words.","Write two criteria that would tell the class the design works.","Write two constraints from the situation."],key:["Something like: the seedlings need water for three days with nobody there to water them. A problem statement, not a solution. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two testable criteria: the soil is still damp after three days, no seedling wilts, the water does not spill on the sill, it can be set up in a few minutes, it uses no electricity. (3-5-ETS1-1)","One afternoon, only jars, string, bottles and a tray, no money to spend. Any two. Giving a criterion here instead is approaching. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Study the three designs. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"generating and comparing solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three designs for watering the seedlings while nobody is there.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>What it is</th><th>Materials</th><th>Set up time</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>a string wick from a jar of water into each pot</td><td>jars and string</td><td>short</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>stand all the pots in a shallow tray of water</td><td>a tray</td><td>very short</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>fit a timer and a small pump on a tap line</td><td>a timer and pump to buy, plus plumbing</td><td>two weeks</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which design breaks a constraint? Name the constraint.","Compare designs 1 and 2 against the criteria. Which do you expect to keep the soil damp without drowning the roots, and why?","Add a fourth idea of your own that fits the materials and the time."],key:["Design 3. It has to be bought and plumbed in and there is no budget, and two weeks is far longer than one afternoon. (3-5-ETS1-2)","The string wick, because it carries water only as fast as the soil draws it, so the soil stays damp without sitting in water. A tray gives the roots as much water as they can soak up and the lowest soil can stay waterlogged for three days, which rots roots. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Any idea in the materials on hand: an upturned bottle with a small hole pushed into the soil, cover the pots loosely with a clear bag to hold the moisture in, move the pots out of direct sun so they dry more slowly, a bottle of water with a wick in its neck. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming a failure point",html:()=>`<p class="big">The class wants to find out whether the string wick or the water tray keeps seedlings healthier over three days.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sprout",96)}${F.icon("drop",96)}</div>`,qs:["Plan the test. What will you measure?","Name two things you must keep the same for both groups so the test is fair.","Name one failure point: where your design is most likely to stop working."],key:["Any measured outcome: how many seedlings are still upright after three days, how heavy each pot is before and after as a measure of water used, how damp the soil is at a set depth, measured the same way for both. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any two: the same number and kind of seedlings, the same pots and the same soil, the same starting water, the same window and the same light, the same three days. Only the watering method should differ. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any sensible weak spot: the wick dries out and stops drawing, the jar runs empty, the string slips out of the soil, the tray dries up or overflows, the soil in the tray goes sour, the pots in the tray have no drainage hole. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:27,title:"High and Low in California",short:"Regions",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart of four California places. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing the physical and human geographic features that define places and regions in California",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four places in California and what the map records for each.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("mountain",80)}${F.icon("cactus",80)}${F.icon("wave",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Place</th><th>Region</th><th>Height</th><th>Something people built there</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Mount Whitney</td><td>Sierra Nevada</td><td>4,421 m, the highest point in the lower 48 states</td><td>a trail to the summit</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Badwater in Death Valley</td><td>Desert</td><td>86 m below sea level, the lowest point in North America</td><td>a road and a boardwalk</td></tr>
     <tr><td>The Golden Gate</td><td>Coast</td><td>sea level, a narrow strait into a large bay</td><td>a bridge and a major port</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Stockton</td><td>Central Valley</td><td>4 m</td><td>an inland port reached by a deep water channel</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How many metres apart in height are Mount Whitney and Badwater? These two places are about 130 kilometres apart.","The Golden Gate is a narrow gap in the coastal hills. Explain how that physical feature led to the human features listed beside it.","Stockton is an inland port, 4 metres above sea level. What had to be built for ships to reach it. Why would people want that?","Name one physical feature and one human feature from this chart, and say what makes each one that kind."],key:["About 4,507 metres, adding 4,421 above sea level to 86 below. Accept a well explained estimate of about 4,500 metres. Partial: 4,421 given with the 86 below sea level left out is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","A narrow strait is the only sea entrance to a large protected bay, so ships must pass through it, which makes it the place to build a port, and the narrow gap is also the place where a bridge can be built across. Partial: one of the port and the bridge explained is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","A deep water channel dredged inland from the bay. It lets ocean ships load valley crops right where they are grown instead of carting everything to the coast first. Partial: naming the channel with no reason people wanted it is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Physical: the peak, the below sea level basin, the strait, the elevations. Human: the trail, the road and boardwalk, the bridge, the ports, the dredged channel. Physical comes from nature, human is built by people. Partial: one of each named with no difference stated is approaching. (HSS-4.1)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Many Nations, One Rush",short:"California Past",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.2"],n:3,dur:170,dir:"Read the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing the peoples of early California and their interactions through the mission and rancho periods",html:()=>`<p class="big">California was home to many separate nations long before 1769, and each period that followed changed their lives.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Nation</th><th>Where they lived</th><th>What they depended on</th><th>What changed after 1769</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Chumash</td><td>southern coast and the Channel Islands</td><td>ocean fishing from plank canoes, shellfish, shell bead trade</td><td>Franciscan missionaries built missions in their territory to convert them to Christianity, and many were brought to live and work at them</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Yokuts</td><td>the Central Valley and its marshes</td><td>fish, waterbirds, tule reeds, acorns</td><td>ranchos and later farms took the valley land and drained the marshes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Mojave</td><td>along the Colorado River in the desert</td><td>farming the river flood plain, long trade walks to the coast</td><td>far from the missions, so change came later, with roads and the railroad</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Each nation also had its own religious beliefs and its own ceremonies, and these were not the same from one nation to the next. Chumash ceremonies were led by trained specialists. Yokuts nations held a mourning ceremony each year for the people who had died since the last one. Mojave accounts of how their world began centre on a mountain near the Colorado River, and those accounts were kept in long song cycles sung from memory.</p>`,qs:["All three nations were in California, yet they lived very differently and their ceremonies were different too. Explain why they lived differently, using the chart, and name one ceremony or belief from one of the three nations.","According to the chart, what did the Franciscan missionaries who built missions in Chumash territory come to do? And why did change come later for the Mojave than for the Chumash?","The Mojave made long trade walks to the coast. What does that tell you about how the nations of California were connected?"],key:["Each nation used what its own place provided. The coast gave fish and shellfish, the valley marshes gave fish, birds and reeds, and the desert river gave flood plain soil for farming. Geography shaped the way of life. Ceremony or belief, any one correctly matched: Chumash ceremonies were led by trained specialists; Yokuts nations held a yearly mourning ceremony; Mojave accounts of how their world began centre on a mountain near the Colorado River and were kept in long sung song cycles. Credit a student who says the three nations did not share one religion. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the geography half with no ceremony or belief named sits there. (HSS-4.2)","They came to convert the Chumash to Christianity, and many Chumash were brought to live and work at the missions. On the Mojave: the missions were built in a line near the coast, where the Chumash lived. The Mojave lived far inland along the Colorado River, well away from that line, so the changes reached them later, with roads and the railroad. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so naming the purpose with nothing on the Mojave sits there. (HSS-4.2)","They were connected by long standing trade routes across the state. Goods, shell beads and news travelled hundreds of miles between nations, so these were not isolated groups. Partial: saying they traded with nothing said about the distance or the goods is approaching. (HSS-4.2)"]},{std:["HSS-4.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"explaining the political and economic life of California from the Bear Flag Republic through the Gold Rush to statehood",html:()=>`<p class="big">California's first state government, and the rush that made it necessary.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1846, Sonoma</td><td>American settlers declared the Bear Flag Republic. It lasted weeks, until the war with Mexico reached California.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1848</td><td>The war ended and California passed to the United States. Gold was found at Coloma.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1849, Monterey</td><td>48 delegates wrote a state constitution. Eight of them had been born in California under Mexican rule, and the finished constitution was printed in both English and Spanish.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1850</td><td>California was admitted as the 31st state.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Population</td><td>About 14,000 non Native residents in 1848, and about 93,000 counted in the 1850 census.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["The constitution was printed in English and Spanish. What does that tell you about California in 1849?","The population went from about 14,000 to about 93,000 in about two years. Name two problems a government would face with that kind of change.","Put these in order and explain the chain: statehood, gold found, the war ends."],key:["Many Californians spoke Spanish and had lived there under Mexican rule, so the new state had to govern two language communities from the start. Eight delegates were Californians born under Mexican rule. Partial: noting the two languages with nothing said about who lived there is approaching. (HSS-4.3)","Any two reasoned problems: no courts or police in the new camps, no clear rules about who owned land or a mining claim, not enough food and housing so prices soared, disputes between people from many countries, no schools, roads or post, no way to collect taxes. Partial: one reasoned problem is approaching, and two problems asserted with no reasoning is approaching too. (HSS-4.3)","The war ended in 1848 and California passed to the United States, gold was found the same year and the population multiplied, and the need for law and government brought statehood in 1850. The gold is the link between the other two. Partial: the three in the right order with no chain explained is approaching. (HSS-4.3)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Growing Cities, Written Rules",short:"Government",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"tracing California's growth into an agricultural and industrial power since the 1850s",html:()=>`<p class="big">California's population, and what was driving the growth.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>People in California</th><th>What was drawing them</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1850</td><td>about 93,000</td><td>gold</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1880</td><td>about 865,000</td><td>the railroad, wheat and fruit farming</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1920</td><td>about 3,400,000</td><td>oil, irrigated farming, early film studios</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1960</td><td>about 15,700,000</td><td>aircraft plants, shipyards, new water projects, new suburbs</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2020</td><td>about 39,500,000</td><td>technology, trade through the ports, services</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Between which two rows in the chart did the population grow the most? Say the numbers.","Pick one row and explain how the reason given in it depended on something built or moved by people.","Each wave of growth needed more water. Connect the population chart to the water projects you know of."],key:["Between 1920 and 1960, from about 3.4 million to about 15.7 million, a rise of more than 12 million, far larger than any other step in the chart. Partial: the right pair of rows named with no numbers is approaching. (HSS-4.4)","Any row with the built part named: the railroad was built; irrigated farming needed canals and dams; film studios and oil fields needed roads, rail and ports; aircraft plants needed runways, factories and electricity; technology and trade need ports, roads and power. Partial: a row named with nothing built or moved by people in it is approaching. (HSS-4.4)","The more people there were, the more water had to be brought from where it falls to where they live. Aqueducts, the Central Valley Project and the State Water Project were all built while the population was multiplying, and the biggest water projects line up with the biggest jumps in people. Partial: saying more people need more water with no project named is approaching. (HSS-4.4)"]},{std:["HSS-4.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"explaining the structures, functions and powers of local, state and federal government",html:()=>`<p class="big">Offices in California government and in the United States government.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Office</th><th>Level</th><th>Branch</th><th>Chosen how</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Mayor</td><td>city</td><td>executive</td><td>elected by city voters</td></tr>
     <tr><td>City council member</td><td>city</td><td>legislative</td><td>elected by city voters</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Governor</td><td>state</td><td>executive</td><td>elected by state voters</td></tr>
     <tr><td>State senator</td><td>state</td><td>legislative</td><td>elected by voters of a district</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Member of Congress</td><td>federal</td><td>legislative</td><td>elected by voters of a district</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Federal judge</td><td>federal</td><td>judicial</td><td>appointed, not elected</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which office in the chart is not elected. Why might the writers of the U.S. Constitution have chosen that?","Name the job of the legislative branch. Name the job of the executive branch. Do it using an office from the chart for each.","A new rule is wanted about dogs in the city park. Which offices in the chart would be involved, and which would not?"],key:["The federal judge, who is appointed. Judges decide what the law means, and the writers wanted them free to rule without having to win the next election, so an unpopular but correct decision would not cost them their job. Partial: the office named with no reason for appointing rather than electing is approaching. (HSS-4.5)","The legislative branch makes laws, such as a city council passing a city rule or Congress passing a national law. The executive branch carries them out, such as a mayor running the city departments or a governor running the state's. Partial: both jobs named with no office from the chart for either is approaching. (HSS-4.5)","The city council would pass the rule and the mayor and city staff would carry it out. The governor, state senator, member of Congress and federal judge would not, because a city park rule is a local matter. Partial: naming who would be involved with no account of who would not is approaching. (HSS-4.5)"]}]}];const WC=CREW.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[0].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level story read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={3:{id:"L3",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Exact Words",std:["RL.5.1"],needs:["RL.4.1"],n:2,dir:"Find the words in the story and read them out loud exactly as they are written.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Read me the exact words from the story that prove the bag was too heavy for Hana.</li><li>The story never says that Hana wants the job again next year. Read me the exact words that show it anyway.</li></ol>${F.passage(HANA,HANA_TITLE,true)}`,key:["Quotes accurately, such as: “The bag was heavier than it looked,” or “Her palms had two red lines across them where the bag handle had been.” The student should read the words as written, not retell them. (RL.5.1, quoting accurately)","Quotes: “because then her mother would carry the bag next year.” That shows she plans to do it again and does not want it taken away. (RL.5.1, quoting to back an inference)"]},4:{id:"L4",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Mixed Units",std:["5.MD.A.1"],needs:["4.MD.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A rope is 250 centimeters long. How many meters is that?</li><li>The crew has three ropes: one 4 meters, one 250 centimeters, and one 1.2 meters. What is the total length in meters?</li><li>A pot holds 2,500 milliliters of cocoa. How many liters is that?</li></ol>`,key:["2.5 meters. 250 ÷ 100 = 2.5. (5.MD.A.1, converting a smaller unit to a larger one)","7.7 meters. 250 centimeters is 2.5 meters, and 4 + 2.5 + 1.2 = 7.7. (5.MD.A.1, a multi-step problem that needs a conversion first)","2.5 liters. 2,500 ÷ 1,000 = 2.5. (5.MD.A.1)"]},7:{id:"L7",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Like a Jump Rope",std:["RL.5.4"],needs:["RL.4.4"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use the story.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>“The sway turned into a slow roll, like a long jump rope turning when two people swing it easy.” Is that a simile or a metaphor? How do you know, and what does it tell you about the rope?</li><li>“A rope pulled tight is a rope that snaps.” Dmitri is not only talking about rope. What else could that line be about, and what makes you think so?</li></ol>${F.passage(WIND,WIND_TITLE,true)}`,key:["A simile. It compares the sway to a turning jump rope using the word “like.” It tells you the rope moved in a big, slow, even curve instead of a sharp whipping motion, the way the middle of a long jump rope swings. If the student argues it is not a true simile because the words like and as are missing, accept that as long as they explain the comparison. (RL.5.4, simile)","It can also mean that anything held too stiffly gives way under strain, so leaving a little room is what keeps it whole. The story sets it next to Lucia letting out line and the lanterns riding the wind out, which shows the loose rope is the one that survives. (RL.5.4, figurative meaning of a phrase in a text)"]},9:{id:"L9",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Three Texts",std:["RI.5.9"],needs:["RI.4.9"],n:2,dir:"Read the third text. Then answer out loud using all three texts.",html:()=>`${threeTexts()}<ol class="qlist"><li>Use all three texts. Explain the whole chain: why a star's look can fool you, and how astronomers get past it.</li><li>Which text would you use to find out how a star's distance is measured, and which two would you use to explain why distance matters so much? Tell why.</li></ol>`,key:["Text 1: light spreads out as it travels, and doubling the distance leaves about a quarter of the light, so distance alone makes a light look faint. Text 2: a star can look bright because it gives off a lot of light or because it is close, so looks alone cannot tell you which. Text 3: astronomers measure the distance first, by watching the star shift against the background six months apart, and then turn how bright it looks into how bright it really is. (RI.5.9)","Text 3 for how the distance is measured, because it is the only one that describes the six month shift. Texts 1 and 2 for why distance matters: Text 1 gives the rule about light spreading out and Text 2 shows that it makes huge far stars look faint. (RI.5.9)"]},11:{id:"L11",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Paired Words",std:["L.5.1"],needs:["L.4.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each sentence out loud the correct way.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Finish it with either and or, or with neither and nor: ____ the tape ____ the spare cord was in the bag. The bag was empty.</li><li>By the time the crowd arrived, the crew ____ for two hours. (work) Start with had been.</li><li>Fix the shift in tense: Last night the crew walked the rope, tapes the worn spots, and hangs the lanterns.</li></ol>`,key:["Neither the tape nor the spare cord was in the bag. (L.5.1, correlative conjunctions)","had been working. (L.5.1, past perfect progressive tense)","Last night the crew walked the rope, taped the worn spots, and hung the lanterns. (L.5.1, correcting a shift in verb tense)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.4.1","RL.4.4","RI.4.1","RI.4.9","W.4.8","W.4.2","RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.9"];const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];const STARTERS={facts:["The rope crew ____.","One thing the crew does is ____.","For example, ____.","Also, ____.","In the end, ____."],story:["By the time the last lantern was lit, ____.","First, ____.","After that, ____.","“____,” I said.","Finally, ____."],"W.4.8":["Text 1 says ____.","Text 2 says ____.","In my own words, ____.","My sources: ____ and ____."],"RI.4.9":["Text 1 says ____.","Text 2 says ____.","Put together, that means ____."],"RL.4.4":["The word ____ means ____.","I know because the story says ____."],"W.4.1":["The town should start the walk ____.","One reason is ____.","For instance, ____.","Another reason is ____.","That is why ____."],"SL.4.2":["In my own words, ____.","The graph shows ____.","Put simply, ____."],"SL.4.4":["I am going to tell you about ____.","First, ____.","One detail is ____.","That is why it matters."]};function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function unitHtml(){return F.table(["1 of the bigger unit","equals","of the smaller unit"],[["1 kilometer","=","1,000 meters"],["1 meter","=","100 centimeters"],["1 kilogram","=","1,000 grams"],["1 pound","=","16 ounces"],["1 liter","=","1,000 milliliters"],["1 hour","=","60 minutes"],["1 minute","=","60 seconds"],["1 foot","=","12 inches"]])+`<p class="small">Going from a bigger unit to a smaller one, multiply. The number of units gets bigger because each unit is smaller.</p>`}function areaHtml(){return F.table(["What to find","How"],[["Area of a rectangle","length × width, answer in square units"],["Perimeter of a rectangle","2 × (length + width), answer in plain units"],["A missing side length","area ÷ the side you already know"]])+`<p class="small">Area counts the squares inside the shape. Perimeter measures the trip around the outside.</p>`}function shapeHtml(){return F.table(["Word","What it means"],[["Parallel sides","run the same direction and never meet, no matter how far they go"],["Perpendicular sides","meet at a square corner"],["Right angle","a square corner, like the corner of this page"],["Right triangle","a triangle with one right angle"],["Parallelogram","four sides, two pairs of parallel sides"],["Trapezoid","four sides, exactly one pair of parallel sides"]])+`<p class="small">Use the corner of a card or a folded paper to test whether an angle is a right angle, smaller, or bigger.</p>`}const SENT_ALIAS={opinion:"W.4.1",facts:"W.4.2",story:"W.4.3","W.4.1":"opinion","W.4.2":"facts","W.4.3":"story"};function sentKey(st){const ps=st&&st.parts&&st.parts.length?st.parts:st?[st]:[];const pick=c=>!c?null:STARTERS[c]?c:SENT_ALIAS[c]&&STARTERS[SENT_ALIAS[c]]?SENT_ALIAS[c]:null;for(const p of ps){const a=pick(p.alt&&p.alt.key||p.wk||p.wt);if(a)return a;for(const c of p.std||[]){const k=pick(c);if(k)return k}}return null}function sentHtml(st){const k=sentKey(st);return`<ul class="starters">${(STARTERS[k]||STARTERS["W.4.8"]).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}const TOOLS={mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["4.OA.A.1","4.NBT.A.1","4.MD.A.1","4.MD.A.3"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const rr=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===rr&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[rr,k];r()})},units:{name:"Measurement unit card",std:["4.MD.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],html:unitHtml},area:{name:"Area and perimeter card",std:["4.MD.A.3"],html:areaHtml},shapes:{name:"Shape word card",std:["4.G.A.2"],html:shapeHtml},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:["W.4.1","W.4.2","W.4.3","W.4.8","RI.4.9","RL.4.4","SL.4.2","SL.4.4"],html:sentHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"mult",std:["4.OA.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the facts, so rate on whether the student can say the comparison in words and write it as an equation, not on the fact itself."},{t:"mult",std:["4.MD.A.1","4.MD.A.3"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the products. Rate on the setup, the unit, and the reasoning."},{t:"units",std:["4.MD.A.1","5.MD.A.1"],msg:"The unit card gives every equivalence the stop asks for, so this stop then shows only whether the student can pick the right operation. Note it on the record sheet."},{t:"area",std:["4.MD.A.3"],msg:"The card gives both formulas and the missing-side method, so count these answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"shapes",std:["4.G.A.2"],msg:"The shape word card defines parallel, perpendicular, right angle, parallelogram, and trapezoid, so the naming questions become supported. Rate on whether the student can find the features on the panels."},{t:"sent",std:["W.4.2","W.4.3"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.4.8"],msg:"The frames show how to label a note and name a source, so count the list of sources as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.4.9","RL.4.4"],msg:"The frames tell the student to pull from each text. Rate on the content of the answer."},{t:"passage",msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"sent",std:["W.4.1"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.4.2"],msg:"The frames tell the student to say it in their own words. Rate on whether the answer really is a paraphrase and not a repeat."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.4.4"],msg:"The frames supply the order of the report, so count the organization check as supported, not independent."},{t:"mult",std:["4.NBT.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart shows the ten times row, so rate on whether the student can explain the place value, not on the product."},{t:"scribe",std:["W.4.1"],msg:"If an adult wrote for the student, the opinion, the reasons and the conclusion still count. The handwriting and the spelling do not."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
